# ===========================================================================
# PREVIEW SUBFOLDER CONFIG  (Apache servers only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Put this file inside the subfolder you created for testing (e.g. /preview),
# then RENAME it to exactly ".htaccess" (with the leading dot, no .txt).
#
# It does two things:
#   1. Stops the parent WordPress rewrite rules from hijacking these static
#      files, so index.html and the other pages serve directly.
#   2. Keeps the preview out of Google while you test.
#
# NOTE: this only works on Apache. If your server is nginx, an .htaccess file
# is ignored and the equivalent has to be set in the server config by whoever
# administers the box. If the preview URL 404s or redirects into WordPress and
# this file made no difference, that's the likely reason — ask your admin, or
# use the external-host route (Netlify / Cloudflare Pages) instead.
# ===========================================================================

# 1) Serve the static files directly; ignore WordPress's front controller.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine Off
</IfModule>
DirectoryIndex index.html

# 2) Don't let search engines index the test copy.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"
</IfModule>

# 3) OPTIONAL — password-protect the preview.
#    Create a .htpasswd file (many free "htpasswd generator" sites will make
#    one for you), upload it somewhere outside the web root, put its absolute
#    path below, and uncomment these four lines.
# AuthType Basic
# AuthName "FLOW Lab preview"
# AuthUserFile /absolute/path/to/.htpasswd
# Require valid-user
